64947509765625, -0.0212554931640625, 0.0029296875, 0.03314208984375, -0.0017910003662109375, 0.01316070556640625, 0.01358795166015625, -0.0229644775390625, 0.004344940185546875, 0.034637451171875, 0.003520965576171875, 0.039825439453125, -0.00604248046875, 0.0103302001953125, -0.00904083251953125, 0.04254150390625, 0.016754150390625, 0.019683837890625, 0.015625, -0.017364501953125, -0.00646209716796875, 0.003696441650390625, -0.0043182373046875, -0.01904296875, 0.037506103515625, 0.01013946533203125, 0.03509521484375, -0.025848388671875, 0.009124755859375, -0.0166473388671875, -0.002117156982421875, 0.01067352294921875, -0.0086669921875, -0.001552581787109375, -0.005001068115234375, -0.03082275390625, 0.030548095703125, -0.02398681640625, -0.0225067138671875, 0.01248931884765625, -0.004642486572265625, 0.01165008544921875, 0.00827789306640625, 0.036407470703125, 0.047454833984375, -0.01270294189453125, -0.0188140869140625, 0.00029969215393066406], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance, and to compare its performance to existing manual review practices.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. The multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three main agents: (1) a structuring agent that segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using a set of regular expression rules and a local large language model (LLM); (2) a consistency-checking agent that detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections and within sections; and (3) a QA agent that identified gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented communication of critical findings. Agents operated sequentially, with outputs from one agent serving as inputs for the next. The workflow was implemented locally for data privacy. For baseline comparison, manual review by radiologists served as the reference standard for structuring and error detection.\n\nTechnical Details: The structuring agent used regular expressions to identify section headers (e.g., \"Lungs:\", \"Liver:\", \"Kidneys:\") and assign sentences accordingly. Sentences not matching predefined headers were processed by a local LLM (based on the Llama-2 architecture, fine-tuned on 5,000 de-identified radiology reports from the same institution). Fine-tuning used an 80/20 training/validation split, a batch size of 16, learning rate of 2e-5, and 3 epochs on an NVIDIA A100 GPU. The LLM was validated on a holdout set of 200 reports, achieving 87% accuracy in section assignment. Regular expressions were iteratively refined based on error analysis.\n\nError Flagging Criteria: A \"section mismatch\" was defined as a clinically relevant finding\u2014specifically, any abnormality or diagnosis with potential to alter patient management (e.g., pulmonary nodule, liver lesion, bowel obstruction)\u2014present in the Findings section but absent or contradictory in the Impression section, or vice versa. For example, a pulmonary nodule described in Findings but not mentioned in Impression, or an Impression stating \"no acute findings\" despite abnormal Findings, constituted a mismatch. A \"gender-anatomy conflict\" was flagged when a report referenced anatomy inconsistent with the patient\u2019s recorded gender (e.g., mention of \"prostate\" in a female patient). \"Undocumented critical findings\" were flagged when a critical finding (e.g., pneumothorax, active hemorrhage) was described in Findings but lacked documentation of communication in the Impression or dedicated communication section. Examples of each error type were reviewed by radiologists to confirm operational definitions.\n\nSampling Strategy: For radiologist evaluation, 45 reports were selected using stratified random sampling to ensure representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) and anatomical regions (chest, abdomen, pelvis). The sample size was determined based on feasibility for expert review and to ensure at least 10 examples of each error type, but is limited in statistical power and may not be fully representative of the broader dataset.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as all sentences accurately assigned to the appropriate anatomical section without loss or fabrication of information, as determined by consensus radiologist review. \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated misassignment of sentences or alteration of clinical content. QA performance ratings were defined as follows: \"excellent\" (no errors, all flagged issues correctly identified, and no unflagged errors), \"good\" (minor errors present, such as a single missed or incorrectly flagged issue not affecting clinical interpretation), and \"fair\" (errors with potential to affect clinical interpretation, such as missed critical findings or major section misassignments). Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring (0.56) and for error flagging (0.61). For error detection, system-level accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated against the radiologist consensus: for section mismatches, accuracy 89%, precision 81%, recall 77%, F1-score 79%; for gender-anatomy conflicts, accuracy 99%, precision 100%, recall 83%, F1-score 91%; for undocumented critical findings, accuracy 98%, precision 80%, recall 75%, F1-score 77%.\n\nResults:\n\nStructuring Agent Performance:\n- The structuring agent processed all 638 reports (22,270 sentences), assigning sentences to predefined anatomical sections while retaining original content.\n- Compared to manual review, the agent achieved 87% accuracy in section assignment on the validation set.\n\nError Types and Flagging:\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%): 80 (12.5%) for section mismatches, 6 (0.9%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 4 (0.6%) for undocumented critical findings.\n- For section mismatches, the most common errors were discrepancies between Findings and Impression sections, such as a pulmonary nodule described in Findings but omitted from Impression.\n- All flagged gender-anatomy conflicts involved references to gender-specific anatomy inconsistent with patient gender.\n- Undocumented critical findings were flagged when critical findings were mentioned in Findings but not documented as communicated.\n- Overall error detection metrics are reported above.\n\nRadiologist Agreement and QA Performance:\n- Both reviewers agreed that 31/45 reports (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%).\n- Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56); for error flagging, kappa = 0.61.\n- Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" in 24/45 reports (53%), \"good\" in 14/45 (31%), and \"fair\" in 7/45 (16%).\n\nError Propagation Analysis: Sequential agent operation could propagate errors from one stage to the next. In the evaluation subset, 3/45 reports (7%) showed evidence of error propagation, where a misassigned section led to a missed or incorrect error flag in subsequent agents. Ablation analysis (removing the structuring agent) reduced overall error detection F1-score by 12%, indicating that structuring accuracy impacts downstream QA performance.\n\nLimitations: This study was limited to a single institution and a specific set of CT reports, which may affect generalizability. The AI system\u2019s performance may vary with different report styles or modalities. The evaluation subset, while stratified, was relatively small and may not capture the full spectrum of report complexity, limiting statistical power and representativeness. Radiologist variability in interpretation and the subjective nature of some QA ratings may introduce bias. Sequential agent operation could propagate errors from one stage to the next, as quantified above. Further validation in larger and more diverse datasets, and benchmarking against other automated approaches, is needed before clinical deployment.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system, comprising structuring, consistency-checking, and QA agents, enabled automated radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system demonstrated moderate accuracy in structuring and QA tasks, with most errors correctly identified and minimal risk of information loss or fabrication in the evaluated sample. While these findings suggest potential for improving standardization and quality assurance in radiology practice, further benchmarking and validation in larger, more diverse datasets are necessary before clinical implementation."}}